{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to take the requirements they infer from existing code, and the changes they propose to existing requirement text, through the review and agreement of {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill` before committing them or tagging code against them. It MUST state that a general request to bootstrap is not agreement to specific text, and MUST direct agents to keep requirements the user rejects or defers on the gap list of {cite}`requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill`. Each area's agreed requirements MUST be committed together before that area's code is tagged.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has four obligations. The one test, `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bootstrap_skill_reviews_inferred_requirements_before_committing_or_tagging]`, covers each of them with assertions on the bundled `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill. I read the skill text and the test helpers (`step`, `step_of`, `assert_text`, `assert_phrases`, `section`) to confirm the assertions check real content.\n\n1. **Inferred requirements, and changes to existing text, go through the `habeas-requirements` review and agreement before committing or tagging.**\n   - The test asserts the skill says \"every requirement starts as a proposal\".\n   - It asserts the \"Propose requirements for the area\" step contains \"proposing any changes to their text\", which covers changes to existing requirement text.\n   - It locates the review step by \"Review, agree, and commit them through habeas-requirements steps 3 to 5\". It then asserts `propose < review && review < tag`, so review comes before the \"Tag the area's tests and root items\" step.\n   - It asserts the review step contains \"don't commit a requirement or tag code against it until the user agrees to its text\", which is the gate on both committing and tagging.\n2. **A general request to bootstrap is not agreement to specific text.** The review step is asserted to contain that exact sentence.\n3. **Rejected or deferred requirements stay on the gap list.** The review step is asserted to contain \"Remove drafts the user rejects, defers, or has not decided from the chapter, and keep them on the gap list\".\n4. **Each area's agreed requirements are committed together before that area's code is tagged.**\n   - The test asserts the Workflow section says \"Repeat steps {propose} to 7 for each area\". This keeps proposing, review and commit, and tagging inside a per-area loop, with review before tagging.\n   - The commit-together part comes from the review step delegating to `habeas-requirements` steps 3 to 5. The test asserts that skill says \"commit it, all groups together\". I confirmed that phrase sits in its step 5, \"Commit\".\n\nThe assertions match the current skill text. For example, step 5 of the skill contains the gate and gap-list sentences. A reviewer could read the commit-together obligation as needing its own statement in the bootstrap skill. The delegation to steps 3 to 5, plus the check on the referenced step, supports the reading that it is met. I did not treat this as a gap.\n\nI found no obligation without a test and no wrong expectation.",
  "references": "8a40a0409c5c9ecf56ef9848c51fa94c27de7bc0bb3ddf59876029b8d53f3674",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill.review",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill.review"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} bootstrap_skill.review",
        "The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to take the requirements they infer from existing code, and the changes they propose to existing requirement text, through the review and agreement of {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill` before committing them or tagging code against them. It MUST state that a general request to bootstrap is not agreement to specific text, and MUST direct agents to keep requirements the user rejects or defers on the gap list of {cite}`requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill`. Each area's agreed requirements MUST be committed together before that area's code is tagged.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} requirements_review_skill",
        "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents adding, changing, or removing end-user-observable behavior that no agreed requirement already specifies in a Habeas project to draft requirements and commit them, once agreed under {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.agreement` and {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.commit`, before writing implementation or test code.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} bootstrap_skill",
        "Habeas MUST bundle a skill for deriving initial requirements from an existing codebase. The skill MUST direct agents to read the code and tests, map every first-party test to at least one requirement or example, identify and map every source unit implementing end-user-visible functionality or interactions to a requirement, and report gaps rather than claim complete coverage without an independent inventory review.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
